(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-06":{"number":6,"title":"Do Not Make Me Guess","content":"In the morning, Greg made coffee as though nothing in the world had changed.\n\nThe coffee maker made a noise like an angry lawn mower, and Greg slapped the side of it with the affectionate impatience of a man who had kept cheap equipment working for years.\n\n“Morning, you beautiful disaster,” he told the machine.\n\n“Are you talking to it or me?”\n\n“Both. One of you owes me a new filter.”\n\nThe joke landed, but there was something underneath it. Last night had happened. I could still feel the impression of his hand at my waist, the almost unbearable tenderness of being wanted without being hurried. In daylight, my borrowed sweatpants on his legs looked ridiculous, and I was suddenly afraid that our happiness would collapse under the weight of ordinary breakfast.\n\nGreg saw me looking at the clothes.\n\n“Just so we're clear, I'm returning these,” he said. “Unless you wanna start a collection of very fashionable ankle pants.”\n\n“Keep them.”\n\nHe set the mug down. “Hey. Did you just give me your only comfortable sweatpants because you think I need a souvenir?”\n\nI hesitated.\n\n“Okay,” he said, softer. “Then let's not make that the rule. I'll bring them back after I wash them.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter rather than approaching me. It gave me space to laugh, to look at him, to decide what I wanted to do. I crossed the kitchen and caught his ridiculous shirt in my fingers.\n\n“Can I kiss you before the machine kills us both?”\n\n“Please. I need something to remember me by.”\n\nHis mouth was warm with coffee, and he laughed when I nearly knocked over the sugar jar. The second kiss was less graceful. I liked it better.\n\nHe stood in my kitchenette wearing the same T-shirt from yesterday and a pair of borrowed sweatpants that were too short in the legs. His hair was sleep-tousled. One hand held a mug; the other tried to operate the ancient coffee maker without making eye contact with the machine.\n\n“It’s judging me,” he said.\n\nI was still in bed, wrapped in the blankets that had seemed perfectly ordinary before the night became something else. “It knows what you did.”\n\nGreg looked over his shoulder. “I made coffee.”\n\n“That’s not what I meant.”\n\n“Oh.” His ears turned pink. “Right.”\n\nThe night returned in fragments: the careful questions, my yes, the way Greg had kissed me as if there were nowhere else he needed to be. There had been laughter, warmth, and an afternoon that ended with the two of us tangled in blankets while the bakery below prepared for the next day.\n\nNothing about it had felt rushed. Nothing had felt taken.\n\nStill, morning had a way of making every decision look larger.\n\nGreg carried two mugs to the bed and sat on the edge, leaving enough room for me to decide whether to close it. I accepted mine. The coffee was sweet and slightly too strong.\n\n“I’m sorry,” he said.\n\nI blinked. “For the coffee?”\n\n“No.” He looked at the mug. “For not asking whether you wanted me to stay.”\n\nI held the coffee between both hands. “You didn’t have to ask.”\n\n“I know. But I should have.”\n\nThe admission was disarming. I had expected him to be cheerful, maybe embarrassed, perhaps distant. I had not expected him to offer an apology before I knew me needed one.\n\n“I wanted you to stay,” I said.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\n“I still want you here,” I continued. “I just don’t know what this is supposed to look like.”\n\n“Neither do I.”\n\n“You seem like somebody who would know.”\n\n“I look very confident from a distance.”\n\n“That’s because your hair has authority.”\n\nHe smiled, then sobered. “We can decide one piece at a time.”\n\n“What piece are we deciding now?”\n\n“Whether you want breakfast.”\n\nThe question made me laugh. Greg looked pleased, but he did not use the moment to avoid the larger conversation.\n\n“Yes,” I said. “I want breakfast.”\n\n“Great. I can make pancakes.”\n\n“Do you know how?”\n\n“I know several shapes.”\n\nHe got up. The room felt different when he moved away, emptier in a way that was not yet painful but could become so if I stared at it.\n\n“Greg.”\n\nHe turned.\n\n“Are you going to disappear?”\n\nThe question came out smaller than I intended.\n\nHe set the mug down. “No.”\n\n“You don’t know that.”\n\n“I know what I’m choosing.”\n\n“People choose things and then change their minds.”\n\n“They do.”\n\n“You might.”\n\n“I might.” He walked back to the bed and crouched so I was looking at each other. “If I change my mind, I’ll tell you. I won’t punish you with silence and call it kindness.”\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n“And if you change yours,” he said, “you tell me. You don’t have to stay because we kissed. You don’t owe me a relationship because I bought a chair.”\n\n“The chair was very nice.”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“I might owe you a little.”\n\n“A cinnamon roll would settle the debt.”\n\nThe tension loosened enough for me to breathe.\n\nGreg stood again and began making pancakes. He hummed while he worked, the melody slipping in and out of tune. I watched his shoulders move beneath the shirt, the easy confidence of his hands, the way he checked the pan before adding batter.\n\n“You’re staring,” he said without turning.\n\n“You’re making breakfast in my kitchen.”\n\n“That’s not a defense.”\n\n“It’s my best one.”\n\n“I accept.”\n\nThe first pancake was too pale. The second was shaped vaguely like a crescent moon. The third landed halfway on the plate.\n\n“You said several shapes,” I reminded him.\n\n“I didn’t say they were edible shapes.”\n\nI ate all three.\n\nAfter breakfast, Greg washed the dishes. He did not put my things away without asking. When he picked up a mug, he paused and looked at me.\n\n“Cabinet or counter?”\n\n“Counter.”\n\n“Good to know.”\n\nThe tiny question touched me more than it should have. I had lived with people who moved through my space as if my preferences were clutter. Greg treated a mug like a negotiation.\n\nWhen he finished, he dried his hands on a towel and faced me.\n\n“There’s something I want to say.”\n\n“That sounds serious.”\n\n“It is, but not bad serious.”\n\nHe leaned against the counter. “I’m attracted to you. I like you. I like what happened yesterday, and I would like it to happen again. But I don’t want you to feel like you have to be easy for me. You can ask for space. You can say no. You can change the plan halfway through.”\n\n“You’re giving me a lot of instructions for someone who just burned a pancake.”\n\n“I contain contradictions.”\n\n“So do I.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nThe warmth returned to his eyes.\n\nI stepped closer. “What if I don’t know what I want until I’m already doing it?”\n\n“Then we check in while we’re doing it.”\n\n“What if I say yes and then panic?”\n\n“Then we stop and panic together, or separately, depending on what you want.”\n\n“What if I don’t say anything?”\n\n“Then I ask.”\n\nI was close enough now to smell coffee and clean soap. Greg did not reach for me.\n\n“What if I want you to kiss me?”\n\n“Then you can tell me.”\n\n“You could ask.”\n\nHis smile was gentle. “Do you want me to?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Can I kiss you?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe kiss was not like yesterday. It did not carry the nervous rush of a first crossing. It was slower, more knowing. Greg's hand settled at my waist only after I touched his shoulder. When I leaned back against the counter, he followed without pressing me there.\n\n“Still okay?” he whispered.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Tell me if that changes.”\n\n“I will.”\n\nHe kissed the corner of my mouth. Then my cheek. Then the place beneath my ear that made my breath catch.\n\n“Still okay?”\n\nI pulled him closer by the front of his shirt. “Ask me one more time and I’m going to start charging you for repetition.”\n\n“I can pay in muffins.”\n\n“Then continue.”\n\nThe morning unfolded in a softer rhythm. There were kisses and laughter, hands resting where they were welcome, and a moment when Greg's forehead pressed against mine while both of us tried to remember how breathing worked.\n\nWe kept the morning soft with kisses, laughter, and a long conversation about what each of us wanted next.\n\nLater, after we had talked ourselves tired, I woke from a nap with Greg asleep beside me. His arm lay across the mattress, not holding me, only near enough that I could reach it. Sunlight moved across the wall.\n\nI had expected regret. Instead, I felt the quiet shock of being safe after wanting something.\n\nGreg woke when I shifted. “Hey.”\n\n“Hey.”\n\n“Did I talk in my sleep?”\n\n“You apologized to the pancakes.”\n\n“They were under a lot of pressure.”\n\nI turned toward him. “What happens now?”\n\nHe looked at the ceiling. “We keep talking. We keep asking. We see if we like each other when we’re tired and annoying.”\n\n“You’re already annoying.”\n\n“Excellent. We’re ahead of schedule.”\n\nI smiled, but the question remained.\n\n“Do you want to call this dating?” I asked.\n\nGreg considered me. “Do you?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“That’s okay.”\n\n“You’re not going to make me guess?”\n\n“No.” He touched the back of my hand. “I want to date you. If you want to date me. If you don’t, I still want to be someone you can call. But I’m not going to pretend I only want friendship if that isn’t true.”\n\nHis directness made my heart beat harder.\n\n“I want to date you,” I said.\n\n“Okay.”\n\n“That’s all?”\n\n“I’m trying to remain calm.”\n\n“You’re failing.”\n\n“I’m doing my best.”\n\nHe kissed my hand. The gesture was almost old-fashioned, and yet it felt less like a performance than a promise to keep showing up.\n\nThat evening, I walked with him to the boardwalk. Greg carried the new chair. I carried the guitar case. The division of weight was not equal, but it was agreed upon. When I reached the sea wall, he set the chair down and waited for me to sit before taking the space beside me.\n\n“I’m going to write a song about this,” he said.\n\n“About the chair?”\n\n“About the way you keep making me ask.”\n\n“That sounds threatening.”\n\n“It’s affectionate.”\n\nThe sunset spread pink and orange across the water. Greg's shoulder rested against mine.\n\n“You know,” I said, “you can tell me what you want too.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n“You keep making space for me,” I continued. “But you’re allowed to take up some.”\n\nThe expression on his face was so tender that I nearly looked away.\n\n“I want to,” he said.\n\n“Then do.”\n\nGreg shifted closer. His hand found mine between the chairs.\n\n“I want to be here,” he said. “With you. I want to learn what your good days look like. I want to make breakfast badly and hear you complain about my furniture. I want to know whether you ever stop looking at exits.”\n\n“Do you want the honest answer?”\n\n“Always.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Then I’ll sit where you can see the door.”\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe ocean did not promise anything. The future remained uncertain. But Greg was beside me, asking instead of assuming, making room instead of taking it.\n\nFor that evening, it was enough.","word_count":1896},"su-19":{"number":19,"title":"The Door Left Open","content":"The door to Greg's van had always been open.\n\nIt was a metaphor, a practical feature, and occasionally a problem when the wind blew sand across the floor. After his long drive, he replaced the faulty latch and installed a small brass hook so it would stay open without banging against the frame.\n\n“You fixed the door,” I said.\n\n“I repaired it.”\n\n“What’s the difference?”\n\n“Fixing sounds like it was broken forever. Repairing means it gets another chance.”\n\nI looked at him. “That sounds like a song.”\n\n“Everything sounds like a song if you’re emotionally unstable enough.”\n\nThe two of us stood outside the van in the early morning. Greg had spent the last week making small changes: a second key, a new mattress, an extra shelf, and a lockbox for documents. None of it made the van a house. It made it more honest about what it was.\n\nI had spent the same week looking at apartments.\n\nNot because I wanted to leave Greg. Because I wanted a place that belonged entirely to me. A studio with a better window. A kitchen that did not share a wall with a bakery mixer. A door I could lock and unlock without anyone else’s permission.\n\nThe apartment I found was three blocks from the car wash.\n\nGreg showed up for the first viewing carrying a tape measure, a packet of crackers and an expression of exaggerated professionalism.\n\n“Welcome to Universe Real Estate. Today's special: one extremely suspicious closet.”\n\n“You're not the agent.”\n\n“That's why my fees are reasonable.”\n\nThe closet door stuck. He pulled it open and revealed a narrow space with a crooked shelf and a stack of paint cans left by the previous tenant.\n\n“Okay,” he said. “Maybe that one's a fixer-upper.”\n\nI watched him measure the distance between the window and the bed nook. He wasn't measuring for his guitar. He was checking whether my desk would fit without blocking the light.\n\n“You know you don't have to approve,” I said.\n\n“I know. I might actually be a little jealous.”\n\n“Of a closet?”\n\n“Of an apartment that gets to see you every morning. Dumb feeling. Doesn't mean I get to change your mind.”\n\nI took his hand. “I'm ethically non-monogamous, Greg. I prefer polyamory. I've told you that before. But having other people I love doesn't make the place I sleep public property. Or yours.”\n\n“Got it.” He considered. “Your apartment gets a vote, and I have to knock.”\n\n“You also get to say when you need your van to be yours.”\n\n“That thing's gonna be relieved. It's been getting very possessive.”\n\nI leaned into him and kissed him in the empty kitchen. His fingers caught at my sleeve; the tape measure fell to the floor with a tremendous clatter. We both jumped and then laughed, and suddenly the apartment felt less like a decision I was making against someone and more like a place I could live.\n\n“You’re moving,” Greg said when I told him.\n\n“I’m moving closer.”\n\n“Those are different things.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe smiled. “I’m proud of you.”\n\nThe words made my chest warm, but not as much as they would have once. I was learning to receive pride without turning it into a debt.\n\nOn moving day, Greg carried boxes. Mara supervised. Steven appeared with a roll of tape and immediately taped his own sleeve to a box.\n\n“Do you need help?” I asked.\n\n“No,” he said, trying to pull free. “I have a system.”\n\n“Your system is attached to the cardboard.”\n\n“It’s a very secure system.”\n\nGreg cut him loose with a pocketknife.\n\nThe new apartment had two windows and a patch of sunlight that crossed the floor in the afternoon. The kitchen was tiny but functional. The bedroom door closed properly. The walls were bare except for the photograph I had brought from the studio.\n\nI placed it on a shelf.\n\nGreg stood behind me. “That looks good.”\n\n“It’s not finished.”\n\n“Neither am I.”\n\n“You’re not allowed to make everything profound.”\n\n“I’ll try being annoying instead.”\n\nHe kissed my temple.\n\nThe move ended with pizza on the floor. Steven told stories about traveling. Mara argued with Greg about whether the living room needed a plant. I leaned against Greg's shoulder while he ate a slice folded in half.\n\nWhen everyone left, the apartment became quiet.\n\nThere were still boxes in the hallway. The bed had not been assembled. My curtains were folded on the floor. But the air belonged to me.\n\nGreg stood by the door with his jacket on.\n\n“You can stay,” I said.\n\nHe looked at me. “Are you sure?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“I have a van.”\n\n“You do.”\n\n“And a perfectly adequate mattress.”\n\n“You are allowed to sleep in a bed.”\n\n“I know. I’m just making sure you’re not inviting me because the apartment feels empty.”\n\nI crossed the room. “It does feel empty.”\n\n“That’s normal.”\n\n“But I’m inviting you because I want you here.”\n\nHe lowered his gaze. “With your own key?”\n\nI reached into my pocket and held it out.\n\nGreg stared at the key.\n\n“You don’t have to take it,” I said.\n\n“I want to.”\n\nHe accepted it. His fingers curled around mine for a moment.\n\n“You can come in without knocking,” I said. “But you still have to knock if the door is closed.”\n\n“That’s fair.”\n\n“You can leave things here.”\n\n“I’ll bring a record player.”\n\n“You can leave one record player here.”\n\n“Two.”\n\n“One and a half.”\n\n“Deal.”\n\nGreg slipped the key onto his ring. It looked small among the others.\n\n“I’m not moving in,” he said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“But I want this to be somewhere we both choose.”\n\n“It is.”\n\nHe took off his jacket and set it over the back of the couch. Then he walked to the boxes and picked one up.\n\n“What’s in this?”\n\n“Books.”\n\n“The dangerous kind?”\n\n“The ones that make you think.”\n\n“I may need supervision.”\n\nI opened the box. He sat on the floor beside me, and the two of us began sorting.\n\nThe first book went on the shelf. The second went on the nightstand. The third Greg claimed for the van.\n\n“You have a house with wheels,” I said.\n\n“Now you have a house with books.”\n\n“It’s a better metaphor.”\n\n“I’m jealous.”\n\nThe evening became a collection of small touches: Greg's knee against mine, my hand brushing his shoulder, the way he leaned closer whenever I laughed.\n\nWhen the last box was sealed, he stood and offered his hand.\n\n“Dance with me?”\n\n“There’s no music.”\n\n“I can hum.”\n\n“That is not music.”\n\n“You’re cruel.”\n\nHe began to hum anyway. It was one of his unfinished melodies, the one about an open door. I put my hands on his shoulders. He placed his at my waist.\n\nI swayed between the boxes.\n\n“You’re a terrible dancer,” I said.\n\n“I’m a good dancer in low light.”\n\n“We are in low light.”\n\n“Then I’m thriving.”\n\n“We’re going to have to finish this tomorrow,” I said.\n\n“The bed or the boxes?”\n\n“Both.”\n\n“I’m very tired.”\n\n“You chose this.”\n\n“I choose you.”\n\nThe words were quiet, but they filled the room.\n\nOutside, Beach City settled into night. The bakery would begin work before dawn. The car wash sign would flicker. The van would wait three blocks away.\n\nAnd my door would be open.\n\nThe following afternoon, Greg left a small box in my apartment.\n\n“What is it?” I asked.\n\n“A housewarming gift.”\n\nInside was a set of hooks shaped like tiny stars. They matched the moon-shaped hooks in the van.\n\n“You are putting your emotional furniture everywhere,” I said.\n\n“I am creating continuity.”\n\n“You are creating a hook empire.”\n\n“A noble empire.”\n\nI installed the hooks beside the door. Greg hung his jacket on one. I hung my scarf on another.\n\n“That is symbolic,” he said.\n\n“It is a hook.”\n\n“Both can be true.”\n\nI looked at him. “You are going to keep coming here?”\n\n“If you want me to.”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Even when the van is parked outside?”\n\n“Especially then.”\n\nHe kissed me in the new living room. The apartment still held the faint scent of cardboard and paint. The couch was slightly too large. The bedroom door stuck when it rained.\n\nIt was mine.\n\nWhen I pulled back, Greg rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“Thank you for making a place for me,” he said.\n\n“Thank you for not trying to fill every room.”\n\n“I brought one record player.”\n\n“And one box of pastries.”\n\n“The pastries are separate.”\n\n“They can stay.”\n\nHe smiled. “Then I will.”\n\nThe next morning, we finally finished assembling the bed. It took three attempts and one argument with the instructions.\n\n“The diagram is wrong,” Greg said.\n\n“The diagram is upside down.”\n\n“That is a type of wrong.”\n\nI laughed until the bed finally stood.\n\nThe room looked ridiculous when it was finished. The bed leaned slightly. The hooks were uneven. The record player sat on a crate because the table had not arrived.\n\nGreg stood in the doorway. “It is perfect.”\n\n“It is functional.”\n\n“That is a form of perfect.”\n\nI walked to him. “You can come here whenever you want.”\n\n“Even if I bring the wrong pastries?”\n\n“Then you will sleep on the couch.”\n\n“A fair system.”\n\nHe kissed me slowly, and the new apartment learned the sound of both my laughter.\n\nThe next morning, Greg left before the bakery opened. He kissed my forehead, collected his jacket from the star-shaped hook, and promised to return after the car wash.\n\n“You have a key,” I reminded him.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Use it.”\n\n“I will.”\n\nWhen he came back, he carried a chair for the bedroom and a pastry for each of us. The chair was sturdy. The pastry was sweet. The arrangement was imperfect and entirely mine.\n\nI placed a second mug beside the first. Greg filled it with coffee and complained that my tea was too hot.\n\n“It is always too hot,” I said.\n\n“You could wait.”\n\n“Waiting is a strategy.”\n\nHe looked pleased. “You remembered.”\n\n“I remember a lot about you,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe kissed me beside the new chair.\n\nThe chair did not complain. The room held its own quiet, warm with the evidence that love could be invited in without surrendering the key.\n\nI slept easily that night.\n\nThe room held us both.","word_count":1722},"su-36":{"number":36,"title":"When We Choose Each Other","content":"The string lights were crooked.\n\nSomebody had left a folded request card on the stage marked *Greg Universe — Love Song*. There was no name, no signature, just a suggestion that he dedicate the last number to “the person who finally got him to settle down.”\n\nHe read it and made a face.\n\n“Wow. They managed to insult my whole life in one sentence.”\n\n“You could leave it out.”\n\n“Definitely will. I like love songs. I also like not having strangers write my relationship status on a napkin.”\n\nHe tucked the card beneath the sound board, away from the public list. When Ellis came to ask about the event, he told her the performance was for the people who'd worked on the square. He could talk about music and the crew. He didn't need to provide a tidy story about being saved by romance.\n\nI touched his wrist under the table.\n\n“I know it's hard when people think being with me means you've given up being on the road.”\n\n“Yeah.” He sighed. “I spent a lot of years running from stuff. Also a lot of years driving to places because I actually wanted to go there. Those aren't the same thing.”\n\n“You don't have to prove you're settled by staying still.”\n\n“Good, because the van would get suspicious.”\n\nHis grin came back unevenly. He drew me against his side long enough for me to hear his heartbeat beneath the ridiculous shirt he insisted was stage wear. I caught the smell of the ocean and the cinnamon frosting from the volunteers' cake.\n\n“You look nervous,” I said.\n\n“I'm about to sing in front of fifty people while you stand there looking at me like that.”\n\n“Like what?”\n\n“Like you already know how the song ends.”\n\nI kissed him behind the sound board. It did not make the lights straighten or the band arrive, but it made the waiting a lot more interesting.\n\nOne loop dipped low enough to brush Greg's hair. Another had been wound around the same post three times because someone had changed their mind about where the party ought to end. The band was late, the paper plates were trying to escape in the wind, and Greg was standing in the middle of the Beach City square with a microphone he had no intention of using.\n\n\"You're staring,\" he said.\n\n\"I'm assessing the danger.\"\n\n\"The lights?\"\n\n\"Your haircut.\"\n\nHe grinned and tucked a loose strand behind his ear.\n\nThe celebration had started as a small gathering after a community cleanup. People had carried away storm debris, repaired the picnic tables, and borrowed the church's folding chairs for the evening. Greg had volunteered music for one set; I had promised to help lay out the food. Neither of us had agreed to be the event's romantic headline.\n\nThen a local newspaper had asked for a photograph.\n\nThe reporter, a young woman named Ellis, approached with a camera and a folded page of interview notes. She looked nervous.\n\n\"Greg, can I get one shot of you and Jasper? People have been talking about you two.\"\n\nGreg's smile thinned, almost invisibly.\n\n\"Depends what the story's about,\" he said.\n\n\"The cleanup. And, well, the relationship. It's kind of sweet. Maybe a little unconventional.\"\n\nI set down a stack of cups.\n\n\"Unconventional how?\"\n\nEllis glanced at her notes.\n\n\"Someone said you travel a lot, Jasper. That you see other people.\"\n\nGreg looked at me, not to make me answer but to check whether I wanted to.\n\n\"I do,\" I said. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. Everyone I'm involved with knows that, and each relationship is based on informed choice.\"\n\nEllis nodded quickly.\n\n\"So you aren't serious with Greg?\"\n\nMy stomach dropped.\n\n\"That's not what I said.\"\n\nShe blushed.\n\n\"Sorry. I didn't mean—\"\n\n\"I know. But I'd like you to ask what we actually mean instead of choosing a story where somebody must be temporary.\"\n\nGreg exhaled.\n\n\"I was about to say something stupid,\" he admitted.\n\n\"What kind?\"\n\n\"Something about being lucky to have whatever time you can spare for me.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"I know. It sounds kind of selfless until you realize it makes me look like a guy standing around hoping you'll pick him.\"\n\nEllis lowered her camera.\n\n\"I can leave the relationship out of the article.\"\n\n\"That would be my preference,\" I said. \"You can write about the cleanup. Ask the people who worked on the drainage ditch. Ask who hauled the chairs. Don't turn our private time into a test for strangers.\"\n\nEllis nodded.\n\nThen she asked, \"Can I photograph the repaired picnic table?\"\n\n\"Please,\" I said.\n\nShe went to find the volunteers.\n\nFor a moment neither Greg nor I spoke. The wind lifted the napkins, and we had to chase them down the path in a ridiculous little scramble.\n\nGreg caught the last one against his chest.\n\n\"Great start,\" he said.\n\n\"You're very dignified.\"\n\n\"I've been told.\"\n\nWe returned to the tables. He straightened the napkins while I tried to decide whether to bring up the line he'd almost said. I didn't want to leave it sitting between us like a broken instrument neither of us wished to touch.\n\n\"About being grateful for whatever time I can spare,\" I said.\n\nHe went still.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you actually feel that way?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" He looked at his hands. \"I don't want to make you smaller. I like that you have places to go and people to see. I also don't want to pretend I never miss you.\"\n\n\"Then tell me when you miss me. That's allowed.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"Yes. I'm your partner, not a holiday rental.\"\n\nHe laughed, startled.\n\n\"Okay. That's fair.\"\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I choose you when I'm here. I also choose to be honest when I have plans elsewhere. Those aren't competing truths.\"\n\nHe set the stack of napkins down.\n\n\"And I can ask for time?\"\n\n\"You can. I can say yes or no. You get to say no to me too.\"\n\nHe looked at me with an expression both embarrassed and intensely fond.\n\n\"Pretty sure I'm going to need practice.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nSteven arrived carrying a cooler, rescued the conversation by tripping over a cable, and promptly asked why the band hadn't started yet.\n\n\"Artistic suspense,\" Greg said.\n\n\"You're not even playing yet.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nSteven shook his head and helped set the cooler down. He had brought enough cold drinks for the volunteers. Greg asked whether he wanted to stay for the music; Steven said yes, then went to help move chairs.\n\nNo one turned him into a witness to our romantic discussion.\n\nA little later Ellis returned with the article draft. She had removed the guesses about our relationship and added the names of the volunteers who had repaired the square. She asked us to check our direct quotations before printing.\n\n\"Better,\" I said.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Look, someone actually wrote about the storm drain. This town is growing up.\"\n\nThe band finally arrived. Their drummer apologized for being late; a delivery truck had blocked the road. Greg helped carry the cases but did not volunteer to manage the whole set. We hung the last string of lights high enough for people to walk beneath without ducking.\n\nWhen the first song began, Greg reached for my hand.\n\n\"Want to dance?\"\n\n\"I thought you'd never ask.\"\n\n\"I'm trying this new thing called asking.\"\n\n\"Very advanced.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me into the uneven little space between the tables. We moved badly at first, bumping shoulders and laughing when the rhythm changed. He didn't pull me closer until I did, and when his thumb brushed my knuckles I felt the warmth of the choice we'd made in front of nobody who needed to know.\n\nAcross the square, the lights shone on the volunteers, the repaired tables, and the people eating too much cake. There was no grand declaration. We didn't ask the band to play a song about us.\n\nBefore we rejoined the crowd, Greg found a quiet place beside the stacked chairs where the music was just a muffled beat through the wall.\n\n“You look nice,” he said.\n\n“You've been looking at me all night.”\n\n“Yeah, but I was trying to decide whether saying it would make me sound like a guy who just discovered compliments.”\n\n“It might.”\n\nHe gave me an embarrassed grin. “Well, then. You look *really* nice.”\n\nI put my palm against his chest. His shirt was warm from setting up amplifiers, and beneath it I felt the quick, startled rhythm of his breath. I liked that he wasn't always smooth. He'd spent years singing to strangers and could still be thrown off balance by one person standing too close.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” I asked.\n\nGreg's smile vanished for a second. Not because he didn't want it. Because he was listening.\n\n“Yeah,” he said softly. “Please.”\n\nI kissed him beneath the crooked string lights. He made a little noise of surprise when I touched the back of his neck, then rested both hands carefully at my waist. There was nothing dramatic about the way we stood together, and yet I could feel how much effort it cost him not to turn tenderness into a joke.\n\nWhen we parted, he rested his forehead against mine.\n\n“Uh,” he said. “I had a very cool thing to say.”\n\n“Did you?”\n\n“Completely gone. I think you broke the lead singer.”\n\n“Can he be repaired?”\n\n“Maybe. Might need another kiss. No promises about the warranty.”\n\nI laughed against his cheek. We stayed another moment until Pilar called for a hand with the chairs. Greg asked if I wanted to return with him, and I did. Later we would close the curtains on the part of the evening that belonged to us. For now, he had volunteered for a cleanup shift, and I didn't mind watching him keep it.\n\n\nWhen Greg kissed me, it was brief and sweet. I leaned against him, and he rested his cheek against my hair.\n\n\"Can we keep doing this?\" he asked.\n\n\"The terrible dancing?\"\n\n\"That, yes. But I meant being honest before I start making jokes instead.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Let's practice.\"\n\nWe left the square after the cleanup shift we'd agreed to, not after every person had gone home. Steven promised to help the last volunteers collect the chairs. Ellis gave us a copy of the revised article, now mostly about the town's flood drains and the people who fixed them.\n\nGreg tucked it under his arm.\n\nAt the van, he stopped and looked back at the crooked lights.\n\n\"Should we fix that loop?\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Really.\"\n\nHe kissed my cheek.\n\nThe next morning we returned with a ladder and a proper set of clips. The lights hung straight for exactly one afternoon before the sea breeze twisted one of them sideways again.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThe crooked loop stayed until the next scheduled repair, and our relationship did not depend on the entire town approving the shape of it.","word_count":1837}},"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-06":{"number":6,"title":"A Song for the Unfinished","content":"The first visitors from Aotearoa reached the Lanternweald on an evening of mist.\n\nTheir crates arrived on a different cart. One was marked with the name of the person responsible for the instruments; another carried tools for repairing a loose frame before anything was performed. The visitor at the back checked each strap before the load was lifted.\n\nMaui eyed the biggest box. “I could carry the entire thing.”\n\nMereana stepped between him and the handle.\n\n“I know you could. Do you know how it's packed?”\n\n“Carefully?”\n\n“Not an answer.”\n\nMini Maui struck a grand pose on his shoulder, then pretended to be crushed by an invisible crate. I had to look away to keep from laughing.\n\nMereana asked her own team to unload the instruments and gave Maui a job they had agreed to: stand at the far end of the path and keep the crowd clear while the fragile case was moved. He looked genuinely disappointed for perhaps three seconds.\n\n“Very well,” he announced. “The great Maui shall guard the path against reckless admirers.”\n\n“Wonderful,” I said. “Try not to start a fan club.”\n\nHe winked. But he moved the crowd, and nobody had to hand over an object they knew how to carry to a stranger who wanted to demonstrate his strength.\n\nThe gathering felt different after that. These visitors had chosen what to bring and what to share, and I wanted the valley to be the sort of place that remembered those choices.\n\nThey came through a Threadway carrying instruments, wrapped food, and stories they had chosen to share. Their organizer, Mereana, introduced herself as a Māori performer and teacher. She spoke carefully about her community, her own teachers, and the fact that no single person could speak for every iwi or every tradition.\n\nMaui tried to greet her with the sort of theatrical flourish he usually reserved for a crowd.\n\nMereana smiled. “I have met impressive storytellers before.”\n\n“Impressive? Sure. But has anybody here persuaded the sun to change its schedule? No? Then I have a truly magnificent introduction prepared.”\n\n“Then perhaps you'll enjoy listening for a change.”\n\nI had to turn away to hide my grin.\n\nThe gathering took place beneath a roof of woven branches beside Lanternwell Square. Mereana spoke of the whare tapere, places associated with storytelling, recreation, music, movement, and meetings between people, including lovers in some traditions. Her group offered a contemporary presentation: a poi performance shaped around a story of separation and reunion, paired with a waiata aroha they had chosen for the evening.\n\nShe explained that waiata aroha could carry longing, grief, or love; poi had its own histories and purposes; and haka was not a generic courtship routine. This evening's piece was their artistic interpretation, not a claim to perform an ancient universal wedding ritual.\n\nI listened to the rhythm of the poi and the steadiness of the performers. The motions were practiced, the story held in a community, not borrowed for someone else's decoration.\n\nBeside me Maui went quiet.\n\nAfterward, Mereana invited questions. Maui asked about the history of the performance with a seriousness that surprised me. He did not pretend that all Polynesian islands shared one identical custom. He listened when she spoke of Aotearoa and of the traditions particular to her people.\n\nMaui's little tattoo raised both arms for an encore that had not been requested. He pressed a broad finger against it, as if that would keep his own enthusiasm from bursting out.\n\nMereana noticed. \"Is that part of your act?\"\n\n\"Only the extremely talented part.\" He grinned. \"The rest of the act is more handsome.\"\n\nShe laughed once. \"We're not doing requests tonight.\"\n\n\"Well, there goes my plan to make a spectacular entrance.\"\n\n\"You're already here.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Wasted opportunity.\"\n\nHe stepped back when her ensemble began packing their own equipment. I could see how hard it was for him not to offer a flourish or take over the carrying. When Mereana asked for help with one marked case, though, he hoisted it as if it were the most valuable treasure on the island and took it exactly where she pointed. He was still Maui; he just hadn't mistaken somebody else's stage for his.\n\nWhen the visitors began serving food, Maui drew me aside toward the edge of the square.\n\n“I had a big speech planned,” he admitted.\n\n“That's shocking.”\n\n“I know. But then I realized it was their night to tell a story.”\n\n“That's almost growth.”\n\nHe put a hand over his heart. “You wound me.”\n\nWe walked between the lanterns while distant voices carried from the gathering. Maui looked at his tattoos.\n\n“Every mark says what I did,” he said. “People think that means they know everything about me.”\n\nI thought of the name I'd found in the spring, and all the rest still hidden.\n\n“Maybe it only means they know where some stories begin.”\n\nHe considered that.\n\nThen he held out his hand.\n\n“Dance?”\n\n“Did you learn that from Mereana?”\n\n“No. I'm asking you to dance with me. Our own steps. No pretending it's somebody else's tradition.”\n\nI took his hand.\n\nHe tried an exaggerated turn that would have looked heroic if he hadn't nearly stepped into a flower bed. I laughed, pulled him back, and found a gentler rhythm on the lanternlit stones.\n\nThere was nothing ceremonial about it. It was clumsy, private in the middle of a public square, and entirely ours.\n\nMaui watched my face as though waiting for me to decide whether I wanted to keep going.\n\nI did.\n\nThe voices from the gathering rose warmly behind us. The people who had shared their art remained its owners. I carried away an understanding, not a souvenir.\n\nAnd in the soft circle of lanternlight, I chose another dance.\n\nOn the way back from the gathering, I stopped beside the little bridge that led to the bakery. One of the paper lanterns had come loose and was turning in the breeze, tapping its wooden frame against a post.\n\nMaui caught it before it fell.\n\n\"Another heroic rescue,\" he announced. \"You may applaud.\"\n\n\"Careful. You'll encourage yourself.\"\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nHe hooked the lantern back into place. His hands were broad enough to make the little brass catch look ridiculous. I watched him test it twice before letting go.\n\n\"What?\" he asked.\n\n\"Nothing. I just like that you checked.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I've been known to take things seriously.\"\n\nBefore I began, I watched Mereana speak with the younger performers while they packed their equipment. One of them asked whether the visitors could repeat a verse outside the planned gathering.\n\n\"We can discuss it with everyone who performed,\" Mereana answered. \"A song offered tonight isn't an invitation for somebody else to decide where we sing tomorrow.\"\n\nMaui went very still beside me.\n\n\"That's fair,\" he said.\n\n\"I thought you'd want an encore.\"\n\n\"I did. But I didn't ask.\"\n\nMereana met his eyes and gave him a small smile. \"You can enjoy what you heard without owning the next performance.\"\n\nI was grateful she'd said it. The evening had been beautiful, and it would have been easy to turn her community's art into scenery for our own relationship.\n\nLater, after the gathering, Maui found me sitting on the low wall by the lanterns. He sat down with enough space between us that it was unmistakably a choice.\n\n\"There's something I don't know how to ask,\" he admitted.\n\n\"Try badly first.\"\n\nHe snorted. \"A very generous invitation.\"\n\n\"I have experience with bad first drafts.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern casting light across the stones. \"If I danced with you, would you think I was asking for more than a dance?\"\n\n\"I'd ask what you meant.\"\n\n\"Which would ruin my mysterious charm.\"\n\n\"Your mysterious charm has never survived five minutes.\"\n\nHe laughed, then sobered. \"I can take an answer that isn't the one I hoped for.\"\n\n\"I hope so. I don't want anybody guessing a promise from a hand held or a song heard.\"\n\nHe nodded. When I told him I wanted to speak openly about how I loved, he didn't interrupt. For once the lanterns and the music were enough of a stage, and neither of us needed the audience to stay.\n\n\"I know.\" I took a breath. \"There's something I want to tell you while we're still figuring out what this is.\"\n\nHis grin wavered. \"That sounds suspiciously like a speech.\"\n\n\"It might be. Try not to interrupt the important bits.\"\n\n\"I'll be magnificent at listening.\"\n\n\"I've never really wanted an exclusive relationship. I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory.\"\n\nHe lowered the arm he'd been preparing to flourish with.\n\nI went on before courage could become an excuse to retreat. \"I mean honesty. People knowing what they're agreeing to. No sneaking around, no promising someone they're the only person I'll ever love and then hoping they never ask questions. Love doesn't feel smaller to me because it has room for more than one person.\"\n\nMaui was quiet long enough that the tide reached the rocks below us and slid back again.\n\n\"More than one person,\" he repeated.\n\n\"Potentially. I'm not asking you to meet anyone. I'm not saying there is somebody else right now. I am saying I won't pretend to want exclusivity because I like you.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern, then at me. \"So the part where I show up and make an idiot of myself doesn't give me the right to decide the rest of your life?\"\n\n\"That part is mostly decorative.\"\n\n\"I suspected.\" His smile returned, smaller this time. \"I don't know all the rules.\"\n\n\"Neither do I. We'd have to make agreements together. You could be jealous. I could be jealous. We'd talk instead of treating feelings like crimes.\"\n\nHe sat on the bridge rail, giving me room beside him. \"I've spent a long time wanting people to pick me. The best. The strongest. The only hero.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I think I'd like to find out who I am when someone chooses me without making a trophy out of it.\" He rubbed the back of his neck. \"I'd want honesty, Jasper. Especially when it makes me nervous. And I might need to ask some truly stupid questions.\"\n\n\"I can survive those.\"\n\n\"Bold claim.\"\n\nI took the seat beside him. Our shoulders did not quite touch. Neither of us moved away.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me before I tried to impress you with another dance,\" he said.\n\n\"You are going to do that anyway.\"\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nHe offered his hand, palm open, without taking mine. I set my fingers in it because I wanted to.\n\nWe stayed there until the lantern stopped rattling.","word_count":1774},"maui-19":{"number":19,"title":"The Apprenticeship of Asking","content":"The boatbuilder was sixteen, furious, and holding a plank upside down.\n\nLio's sketch was pinned beside the slipway. It was covered in revisions, including a note in thick chalk: *Sela's crate must not slide*. That was the part I looked at while Maui looked at the shape of the prow.\n\n“Very elegant,” he said.\n\nLio did not look impressed. “It's a working boat. It needs to stay upright when the water moves.”\n\n“I know something about water.”\n\n“Then you know it won't care how famous you are.”\n\nMaui went silent. Mini Maui on his arm put its tiny hands over its face, which was not helping.\n\nFen came down the steps with the original order. “Lio is responsible for the design review. Maui, you are here as an assistant unless the apprentice asks you to demonstrate.”\n\n“I have sailed across oceans!”\n\n“And you're very welcome to discuss that after the safety checks.”\n\nLio was only sixteen. I could see how much courage it cost them to say no to somebody who filled half the doorway and half the valley's stories. They did it anyway.\n\n“Please hold the plank level while I mark the second support,” Lio said.\n\nMaui reached for the wood, then stopped with his hand open.\n\n“Like this?”\n\n“Lower. No—there. Keep it there.”\n\nHe obeyed. It was a small thing, a demigod being told where to put his hands, and it held more value than another tale about him saving somebody who hadn't needed saving.\n\nI put the measurements into the workshop ledger. There would be time to ask him about the ocean later. Today the boat belonged to its builder and the person who had commissioned it.\n\nI knew the plank was upside down because Fen had marked the side facing the water in bright blue chalk. The apprentice knew it too. They had been staring at the mark for nearly a minute, as though determination might turn the wood.\n\nMaui reached for the plank.\n\n\"Here! I know boats. Hand me that and I'll make it look easy,\" he said, reaching for the plank before Lio had even answered. His grin was all confidence, which would have been more persuasive if the apprentice hadn't looked ready to bite him.\n\nLio pulled it away.\n\n\"I can do it.\"\n\n\"I know. But it'll be faster if—\"\n\n\"I said I can do it.\"\n\nI was carrying a bucket of clean water and trying very hard not to interfere.\n\nA moment later I interfered.\n\n\"Turn it clockwise,\" I said.\n\nLio turned toward me.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nI closed my mouth.\n\nFen watched the three of us with the expression of someone considering whether the dock needed apprentices, heroes, or a large sign prohibiting both.\n\n\"Start again,\" Fen said.\n\nLio put the plank on the sawhorses.\n\nMaui stepped back. So did I.\n\nThe new boat was a shallow coastal skiff intended for short journeys between the Tideglass landing and the sheltered inlet. It wasn't a legendary vessel. It wouldn't make anyone famous. It would carry crates of tea, rope, and occasionally the people who had jobs too small for a great voyage but too important to miss.\n\nLio had been apprenticed to Fen for six weeks. They knew how to measure the ribs, cut a clean mortise, and keep sharp tools out of other people's reach. They also knew, in the way teenagers sometimes know things very loudly, that they had to prove everything without assistance.\n\nMaui had volunteered to teach the knotwork. He had not been asked to build the boat.\n\nHe was having trouble understanding the distinction.\n\nBy midday Lio had finished fitting one rib and rejected four pieces of advice. Maui had demonstrated the same knot eight times. I had corrected two measurements that hadn't needed correcting.\n\nMaui knelt beside the practice frame and demonstrated the knot again. His hands moved with astonishing speed. The rope became a neat loop before Lio had managed to locate the working end.\n\n\"See? Beautiful. Effortless. You may applaud.\"\n\nLio folded their arms.\n\n\"Or,\" Fen said from behind us, \"you could teach a person instead of auditioning for your own statue.\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his forearm and pointed at Lio's empty hands.\n\n\"You're all against me,\" Maui said.\n\n\"Only your teaching method,\" Fen replied.\n\nHe stared at the rope, thinking. Then he untied the knot and tossed the line back to Lio.\n\n\"All right, apprentice. Here's the trick: you tell me what you think the rope wants to do, and I'll tell you how badly you're lying to it.\"\n\n\"Ropes don't want things,\" Lio said.\n\n\"Then why do they spend their lives trying to escape us?\"\n\nLio laughed. They tried again, slowly. Maui kept his own hands to himself and narrated the mistakes with such extravagant descriptions that by the end of the hour the apprentice was calling every tangled loop a *demigod disaster*. Maui pretended to be offended, but he finally looked like a teacher who was having fun watching someone else learn.\n\nFen called a break.\n\n\"Both of you,\" they said, pointing to Maui and me, \"come with me.\"\n\nWe followed them to the workshop.\n\nThey shut the door.\n\n\"Maui, what are you teaching Lio?\"\n\n\"How to tie the rib lashings.\"\n\n\"No. You're showing them how quickly Maui can tie rib lashings.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\nFen looked at me.\n\n\"And you?\"\n\n\"I'm helping with measurements.\"\n\n\"You're taking the pencil every time the numbers look uncertain. What does Lio learn from that?\"\n\nI stared at my hands.\n\n\"Not much.\"\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"This is an apprenticeship. They need to make mistakes within safe limits. We stop them before a mistake harms someone. We don't stop them because the work is taking longer than we like.\"\n\nMaui crossed his arms.\n\n\"What if the boat sinks?\"\n\n\"Then we don't launch it until it passes inspection.\"\n\nThat seemed obvious once someone said it.\n\nI thought about the last several months. Maui and I had spent so much time talking about respecting other people's choices that I'd become proud of how well I could explain it.\n\nExplaining wasn't the same as practicing.\n\n\"What should we do?\" I asked.\n\nFen handed me a blank inspection sheet.\n\n\"Ask Lio what help they want. Then give it.\"\n\nMaui looked at the paper.\n\n\"That's all?\"\n\n\"That's a surprising amount of work for people who enjoy talking.\"\n\nWe returned to the slipway.\n\nLio was sanding the rib we'd left on the sawhorses. Their jaw was set. They didn't look up when we approached.\n\nMaui stopped at a respectful distance.\n\n\"I think I've been showing off,\" he said.\n\nLio glanced up.\n\n\"You think?\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThey looked startled.\n\nHe held out the rope.\n\n\"Would you like me to demonstrate once and then let you try? Or would you rather work it out?\"\n\nLio considered the question.\n\n\"Demonstrate once. Slowly.\"\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"Slowly. I can do that.\"\n\nI tried not to laugh.\n\nHe took nearly a full minute to tie the knot. It was the least dramatic demonstration I'd ever seen from him. He named each loop, explained which part carried the load, and showed how the line could be loosened for inspection.\n\nThen he handed the rope over.\n\nLio got it wrong.\n\nHe waited.\n\nThey tried again.\n\nThis time it held.\n\n\"Good,\" Maui said.\n\nLio grinned before they could stop themself.\n\nI looked down at my inspection sheet.\n\nThe next step was mine. When Lio asked me to check a measurement, I didn't take the pencil. I asked them to read the numbers aloud. We compared the marked board with the drawing and found one discrepancy together.\n\nIt took longer.\n\nIt was better.\n\nAfter lunch we met with the person who had ordered the skiff. A market gardener named Sela needed a shallow boat for moving small tools during flood season. She wanted a low step, a stable handhold, and space for a crate that wouldn't slide when the deck got wet.\n\nLio had designed the boat with a lovely sweeping curve that would leave very little flat space.\n\nThey looked at the drawing, then at Sela.\n\n\"I could change the seat,\" they said.\n\n\"Would that weaken it?\" she asked.\n\n\"Not if I adjust the support.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth.\n\nHe closed it again.\n\nLio looked at him.\n\n\"Would you check the lashing after I've made the change?\"\n\n\"Gladly.\"\n\nIt was a tiny moment, and it felt like something important had shifted.\n\nThe work continued for another week. We didn't suddenly become perfect mentors. Once, Maui started lifting a whole rib into place before Lio had asked for help. Once, I corrected a label that had merely been written differently from the way I preferred.\n\nBoth times Fen raised an eyebrow.\n\nBoth times we stopped.\n\nWhen the skiff was finally ready for the first water test, the whole cove gathered despite Lio's request for a small audience. The line of people along the shore looked like a festival crowd.\n\nLio stared at them.\n\n\"I don't want a speech,\" they muttered.\n\nMaui was holding a rolled piece of paper.\n\nHe looked down at it.\n\nThen at Lio.\n\n\"No speech,\" he agreed.\n\nHe put the paper away.\n\nI loved him a little more for that.\n\nThe boat went into the water carefully. Fen checked the trim. Tamsin measured how low it sat with the first crates aboard. Sela stepped onto the deck and tested the handhold. It was not perfect. The second seat needed to move an inch, and the floor got slippery when a wave came over the bow.\n\nLio wrote both things down.\n\n\"Can I fix them before we call it finished?\" they asked.\n\nFen smiled.\n\n\"That's the point.\"\n\nWe ate bread on the shore afterward. Maui had sand in his hair and a smear of chalk along his wrist. He looked tired in a way that made me think he'd actually been working instead of performing.\n\n\"How was teaching?\" I asked.\n\n\"Harder than fighting monsters.\"\n\n\"Less applause.\"\n\n\"Far less.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"How was not helping?\"\n\n\"Terrible.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI leaned against his shoulder. The skiff rocked softly against its line, unfinished but real.\n\n\"When I first met you,\" I said, \"I thought you were the person who knew what to do.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think the best thing you know is how to learn.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead.\n\n\"Don't spread that around. It'll ruin my reputation.\"\n\nI grinned.\n\nLio called from the slipway.\n\n\"Maui! I need help with the final knot!\"\n\nMaui stood, then paused.\n\n\"Do you want me to tie it?\" he called.\n\n\"No. I want you to watch.\"\n\nHis smile changed.\n\n\"Coming.\"\n\nI watched him go, carrying nothing but his patience.","word_count":1773},"maui-36":{"number":36,"title":"Permission for Privacy","content":"The chalkboard in Lanternwell Square had acquired a new column.\n\nI had expected strangers to speculate about me. I hadn't expected the handwriting to be so familiar.\n\nIt was the same chalk that Mara used to mark the price of bread, though the letters were narrower and the hearts drawn with an impatient twist at the bottom. Whoever had done it had stood here long enough to copy names from overheard conversations.\n\nA young customer came out of the bakery with a roll in their hand. They looked at the board, then at me, and opened their mouth.\n\n“Don't,” Mara said from the doorway.\n\nThe customer flushed. “I was only going to ask—”\n\n“I know. You can ask whether the bread has nuts. You cannot ask whom somebody kissed last night.”\n\nI felt my throat tighten. I hadn't expected the defense, and I wasn't sure whether I wanted to be grateful for it or furious that it was necessary.\n\nMaui appeared beside the fountain just as I reached for the cloth. His expression changed when he read the names. For once he didn't immediately perform anger for the square. He watched me.\n\n“I can make the person who wrote it admit they're wrong,” he said quietly.\n\n“I know you can. I don't need a confession in the middle of the market.”\n\n“Then what do you need?”\n\nI wiped one of the hearts away. A faint ghost of the line remained on the slate.\n\n“I need people to leave the people I love out of their entertainment.”\n\nHe took the bucket from the ground and held it steady while I cleaned the rest. The work was beneath his usual idea of a heroic undertaking, which was exactly why it helped. I could feel how badly he wanted to do more. I also felt him choose not to turn that wanting into a performance.\n\nIt stood beside the bakery menu under the heading *Who's with Jasper?* Someone had drawn six little hearts and written the names of people I cared about, with blank spaces for dates and guesses.\n\nI stared until the letters stopped making sense.\n\nMara came out carrying a tray of rolls. She saw the board and went still.\n\n\"That's not mine.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I was going to wipe it off, but I thought you should decide whether you wanted to see it first.\"\n\nI swallowed. I was furious with the person who'd written it, but I was also strangely ashamed. The chalkboard made my life look like a game people could follow, score, and argue about over breakfast.\n\n\"Would you give me the cloth?\" I asked.\n\nMara handed it over.\n\nI wiped the names away.\n\nThe damp cloth left a grey-pink stain where the little hearts had been. It had also erased half the bakery's prices. A woman waiting for flour squinted at the board and asked how much a small loaf cost.\n\nMara looked at the missing figures. \"I'll put them back. You shouldn't have to redraw the whole market because somebody made a game out of your life.\"\n\n\"Could we have a separate board?\" I asked. \"For actual notices?\"\n\nPip was fixing a crooked lantern overhead. They climbed down to inspect the chalk marks.\n\n\"I can paint one,\" they said. \"But if strangers use my work as a rumor wall, I get to refuse to repaint it every morning for free.\"\n\nMara named a cost for materials from the market fund. Neri offered to put it on the next notice-board agenda. None of the work required explaining my relationships to a single shopper.\n\nMaui's expression sharpened. \"I could rip the old one off the wall. Problem solved.\"\n\n\"Then Mara has nowhere to list breakfast,\" I said.\n\n\"Right.\" He looked disappointed. \"Bad plan. I could guard the new one.\"\n\n\"From what?\"\n\n\"Terrible handwriting, obviously.\"\n\nPip pointed to his own enormous signature on a previous poster.\n\nMaui stared at it. \"That is magnificent handwriting.\"\n\nI laughed despite my anger. The square still needed bread prices, tide warnings and a way for people to ask for help. What it did not need was an audience for my private life.\n\nThe council could decide how the public board was used. I would decide whether, when and how I talked about the people I loved.\n\nBehind me someone said, \"Oh. I thought it was just a joke.\"\n\nI turned. A man I recognized from the market stood holding a basket of apples. He looked embarrassed rather than cruel.\n\n\"Whose joke?\" I asked.\n\n\"People were talking. You and Maui, and the others. We wondered how it all worked.\"\n\n\"Then you could have asked me a question that didn't require exposing six people's private lives.\"\n\nHe shifted the basket. \"I didn't think anyone would mind.\"\n\n\"You didn't ask them.\"\n\nI heard my voice grow sharper. I wanted to make him feel exactly as small as the board had made me feel. I was not proud of that.\n\nMaui arrived before I could say anything else. He looked at the damp board, then at my face.\n\nHis eyes followed the six hearts down the board. I watched the easy grin drain out of his face.\n\n\"Well,\" he said, very softly. \"Whoever drew this has a powerful imagination and absolutely terrible manners.\"\n\nMini Maui came running across his upper arm carrying an imaginary giant eraser. The real Maui looked ready to do the same thing with his hook and perhaps the entire board.\n\nI wanted that anger on my side. I didn't want him to turn it into a public spectacle that would make the story even harder to escape.\n\n\"Jasper,\" he said, voice suddenly big enough to make passersby turn, \"I could give a speech so magnificent they would never gossip again.\"\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\nHe closed his mouth, clearly in pain.\n\n\"Fine. No speech. Extremely difficult, but fine.\"\n\nI saw the impulse hadn't gone anywhere. His fists were tight, and he was watching the man who'd drawn the board. But when I began to speak, he backed up and let people hear me. He wasn't calm. He was choosing not to make his anger more important than mine.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nI told him.\n\nHis jaw tightened. For a moment I saw the old Maui, ready to turn the whole square into a battlefield because someone had embarrassed a person he loved.\n\nHe looked at me. \"What do you want?\"\n\n\"Not a speech from you,\" I said quickly. \"Please.\"\n\nHe breathed in, then out. \"All right.\"\n\nI asked Neri for five minutes at the community notice board. A few people gathered, including several who had seen the chalkboard before it was erased. I kept the account plain.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. The people in my relationships know that. They each make their own choices, and some of those choices are private.\"\n\nA woman raised her hand. \"So you don't have to tell everyone?\"\n\n\"No. Neither do you.\"\n\n\"What about fairness?\"\n\n\"Fairness means honesty with the people affected, respecting consent, and keeping the commitments we actually make. It doesn't mean putting everyone's schedule on a public board.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether my partners liked each other.\n\n\"That's their business,\" I said. \"Some have worked together. Some want space. None of them owes the square a performance.\"\n\nThe man with the apples had stayed near the edge of the crowd. He looked down at his basket.\n\nI could have called him out by name.\n\nI didn't.\n\nInstead I asked the council to put a privacy rule beside the public festival guidelines: notice boards were for community business, not speculation about residents' romantic lives. Mara added a second rule about not writing personal contact information without permission.\n\nNeri approved the notice.\n\nIt wasn't a law against gossip. People would still talk. But it established a boundary the community could enforce in the spaces it maintained.\n\nLater, I found Maui at Tideglass repairing a lantern hook.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\n\"At the board.\"\n\nI leaned against the post. \"You didn't say much.\"\n\n\"You asked me not to.\"\n\n\"Was that difficult?\"\n\n\"Extremely. I had three speeches ready.\"\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nHe put down the hook.\n\n\"About the speech you heroically saved me from giving,\" Maui said. \"Does this mean if somebody asks what we do at home, I can tell them to mind their own coconuts?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I don't want to hear every detail about the others. Sometimes I do need to know when our plans change. And sometimes—\" He scowled at the hook. \"Sometimes my brain insists I should *win* at being loved. Which is ridiculous. And terrible for my winning record.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You can ask. I can decide what belongs to me to share. If it belongs to someone else too, I ask them first.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"What about kisses?\"\n\n\"Those are ours when they're between us. We can talk about them if we both want. They're not the village's property.\"\n\nHe smiled slowly.\n\nMara came back across the square with a fresh price board, still looking upset about what someone had written on the chalk.\n\n\"I was thinking,\" she said. \"Maybe the notice boards should be separated. People keep putting personal messages beside the public safety information because it's the only place everybody knows to look.\"\n\nNeri, who had been updating the departure instructions, stopped to listen.\n\n\"A public board for services,\" they said. \"A private message shelf for things addressed to one person. The shelf would need a way to refuse unwanted notes, too.\"\n\n\"And no list of who collects what,\" I added.\n\nMara nodded. \"I don't want the bakery becoming a gossip desk.\"\n\nWe worked out a small plan that afternoon. The service board would hold prices, closures, community meeting times and voluntarily credited work. Personal messages could be left in sealed envelopes through the registrar, who could return anything unwanted without posting it in public.\n\nPip offered to paint a sign. Edda made them check that the new shelf could be reached without blocking the walkway. A new rule wasn't finished just because someone had written it in attractive letters.\n\nMaui held the ladder while the sign went up.\n\n\"I could give a speech about everyone's freedom,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Would it be bad?\"\n\n\"Probably magnificent.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"And unnecessary.\"\n\nHe sighed theatrically, but let Pip fasten the last corner. When the square was tidy again, he reached for my hand and waited.\n\n\"I like that you can tell me what you want private,\" he said.\n\n\"Even when you want to know?\"\n\n\"Especially then. I may still be curious.\"\n\n\"I'll still tell you the things that affect us. You don't need a public scoreboard to know I care.\"\n\nWe stood beneath the new sign while people walked past without noticing us. It was wonderfully ordinary. The next time somebody wanted to announce who Jasper had kissed, the town at least had a clear reason to say that wasn't what the public board was for.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThe sunset lit the signal post in amber. I could hear Lio arguing with Fen about a skiff measurement and the bakery bell sounding faintly from the square. There was a whole life around us, full of people who deserved to have some parts remain their own.\n\nMaui stepped closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you somewhere nobody's taking notes?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe went home by the upper path, laughing when the wind tried to steal his cloak. At the door he kissed me, then again after I pulled him closer. The rest of the evening was private, the door closed because we had chosen it.\n\nBefore we left, Neri caught us beside the fountain.\n\n\"I have a problem,\" they said, carrying a second sheet of paper. \"We keep an arrival book so people can be found when crossings go wrong. If we stop taking names, we lose the safety record. But if we publish who went where—\"\n\n\"We do the chalkboard all over again,\" I finished.\n\nEdda, passing toward the archive, stopped to listen. She pointed out that the registrar's desk couldn't be reached without stairs after the side path flooded. That made an emergency contact fairly useless if the person who needed the record couldn't get inside.\n\nWe wrote a new rule together. The public board would show Threadway closures and available services, not visitors' destinations. A traveler could give Neri a restricted emergency contact, refuse to list an unrelated private appointment, and request correction without broadcasting their business. If someone challenged an entry, a second registrar would review it.\n\nMaui studied the page. \"That sounds like a lot of work just to stop people being nosy.\"\n\n\"It's work we should have done before the board hurt somebody,\" I said.\n\nHe didn't argue. Instead he held the ladder while Neri pinned the notice in a place Edda could actually read.\n\nThe man with the apples approached again.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he said. \"I made it a joke because I didn't think about the people underneath it.\"\n\nI was still angry. \"Don't do it again.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI wasn't required to make him feel better. He went back to his market stall, and I went home with Maui.\n\nOn the path, Maui touched my wrist. \"Ask about the ledger tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Ask about your other partners?\"\n\n\"Only what affects our agreements, like we said.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I am becoming a very sophisticated man.\"\n\n\"You held a ladder.\"\n\n\"Heroically.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek, which managed to shut him up for nearly ten seconds.\n\n\nThe next morning the chalkboard held the day's bread prices and the tide warning, nothing else.\n\nThere were six hearts still faintly visible beneath the damp surface.\n\nI didn't erase them again.\n\nThe boundary was the important part. The trace of somebody's mistake could stay without deciding what happened next.","word_count":2302}}};for(const s of lib.series){const map=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const e=map[ch.id];if(e&&ch.chapter_number===e.number){ch.title=e.title;ch.content=e.content;ch.word_count=e.word_count;}}s.total_word_count=s.chapters.reduce((sum,ch)=>sum+(Number(ch.word_count)||0),0);}})();
